{"name":"Apricot Froth","hex":"#ffc7a0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"apricot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A round sweet and juicy stone fruit, resembling peach or plum in taste, with a yellow-orange flesh, lightly fuzzy skin and a large seed inside.","The apricot tree, Prunus armeniaca."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a pale yellowish-orange colour, like that of an apricot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/apricot"},{"term":"froth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Foam.","Unimportant or insubstantial talk, events, or actions; drivel."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To create froth in (a liquid).","(of a liquid) To bubble."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/froth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Apricot","blocks":[{"text":"An apricot is a fruit, or the tree that bears the fruit, of several species in the genus Prunus. Usually an apricot is from the species Prunus armeniaca, but the fruits of the other species in Prunus sect. Armeniaca are also called apricots. In 2023, world production of apricots was 3.7 million tonnes, led by Turkey with 20% of the total."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Apricot_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Apricot_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apricot","wikidata":"Q3733836"},{"title":"Foam","blocks":[{"text":"Foam is a two-phase material system and a type of dispersed media, consisting of gas cells dispersed in a continuous liquid or solid phase to form a cellular structure. In most foams, the gas occupies most of the volume and is divided into bubbles separated by thin films of liquid or solid material."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/The_top_of_foamy_drink_%28Unsplash%29.jpg/330px-The_top_of_foamy_drink_%28Unsplash%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbn